    {
      "heading": "The rule of 72",
      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "There's a shortcut to feel this in your head. It's called the rule of seventy-two. Divide seventy-two by the interest rate, and you get roughly how many years it takes for money to double.",
          "show": {
            "type": "note",
            "text": "72 ÷ rate = years to *double*",
            "source": "The rule of 72 – a close approximation",
            "color": "gold"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "At two percent, doubling takes about thirty-six years. At four percent, about eighteen.",
          "show": {
            "type": "columns",
            "heading": "Years to double, rule of 72",
            "stacks": [
              [
                "2%",
                36,
                "36 yrs"
              ],
              [
                "4%",
                18,
                "18 yrs"
              ],
              [
                "7%",
                10.3,
                "~10 yrs"
              ],
              [
                "10%",
                7.2,
                "~7 yrs"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "At seven percent, about ten years. At ten percent, a bit over seven."
        },
        {
          "say": "And here's why that matters. Ten thousand dollars at seven percent doubles to twenty thousand in ten years. Then forty in twenty years. Then about eighty in thirty. And by year forty, close to a hundred and fifty.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "10 → 20 → 40 → 80",
            "caption": "$ thousand, roughly every 10 years at 7%",
            "color": "green"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Each doubling is bigger than all the ones before it put together."
        },
        {
          "say": "The rule also works backwards. If something doubled in about seven years, it grew roughly ten percent a year. If it took thirty-six years, about two percent."
        }
      ]
    },
