      "heading": "The hidden tax: inflation",
      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "There's one more compounding force, and it works on everyone: rising prices.",
          "show": {
            "type": "line",
            "heading": "US consumer prices, 1984 = 100",
            "unit": "",
            "sub": "Consumer price index, rebased · World Bank",
            "series": [
              {
                "label": "",
                "wb": "FP.CPI.TOTL",
                "country": "USA",
                "from": 1984,
                "to": 2024,
                "rebase": [
                  1984,
                  100
                ],
                "color": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "From 1984 to 2024, US prices rose on average about two point eight percent a year. That sounds small."
        },
        {
          "say": "But compounded over forty years, it tripled prices. What cost a hundred dollars in 1984 cost about three hundred and two in 2024.",
          "mark": {
            "x": 2024
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "So go back to our hundred and fifty thousand dollars after forty years. If prices rose as fast as they did in those forty years, it would buy only what about fifty thousand dollars bought at the start.",
          "show": {
            "type": "compare",
            "heading": "$149,745 after 40 years, in starting-year dollars",
            "left": [
              "On paper",
              "$149,745"
            ],
            "right": [
              "After 1984–2024 US inflation",
              "$49,600"
            ],
            "color": "red"
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "That's why economists talk about the real rate: what you earn minus inflation. Money that earns less than inflation shrinks – slowly, and then a lot."
        },
        {
          "say": "The rule of seventy-two works here too. At two point eight percent, prices double in about twenty-six years. If they kept rising at that pace, someone retiring at sixty-five would see prices roughly double before turning ninety.",
          "show": {
            "type": "number",
            "big": "72 ÷ 2.8 ≈ 26",
            "caption": "years for prices to double at 2.8% inflation",
            "color": "red"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
